On 25 Mar 1997 20:48:09 GMT Dirkson E. Bussiere-42T (bussierd@tvsfrank) wrote:
>Here's a quick question for y'all.  We have a soon to be
>3 processor alpha server here and would like to modify
>one of our programs to use all available processors (i.e.,
>vectorize or parallelize it).  On the SGI's, one just sets
>the MIP flag and perhaps set a few DOALLS to tell the compiler
>what the vectorize and the f77 compiler spits out functional
>code.  However, it appears that on the alpha you have to
>buy Fortran 90 and the HPF option to be able to mp your fortran
>code.  Is this true, or is there a work-around for this?
>I'd rather not have to buy another expensive compiler.

Depends.  If your code uses the dxml library (not free, but you may
have it - it contains BLAS routines, FFTs, er, and some other stuff),
or you can rewrite it to use these, then you can just link to libdxmlp
instead of libdxml and Bob's your uncle.  
Or, if your process is multithreaded, then I think 
the different threads will sit on
different processors of their own accord.

I've generally found that the kind of parallelisation where you just
say "yes I'd like it parallelised" and the compiler spits out 
parallel code to run is pretty inefficient, but maybe your codes are
better behaved than mine.

