> Markku Savela <msa@anise.tte.vtt.fi> wrote:
> : In article <5g6388$3l1@hades.rz.uni-sb.de> Christian Blug <et11hkc@levpp1.ee.uni-sb.de> writes:
> 
> :    I'm running into troubles with my new digital alpha 255/233 with
> :    OSF1 V4.0 564 alpha
> :    as OS. I'm using the digital c compiler with gnu gdb 4.16. After starting
> :    the debugger, setting the breakpoint and running the program, the system
> :    crashes with a kernel fault error!
> 
> : I have observed the same thing, but I don't need gdb for this. Just
> : using Digitals own dxladebug on any simple "Hello World" program
> : just panics the kernel (or actually, first crashes the X server, and
> : if you try to run debug on another X display from Alpha, you get
> : kernel panic).
> 
> : Likewise, would like to get a fix for this.

The following *should* be a work around for this problem (assuming that
you are booted off of /vmunix).

  dbx -k /vmunix
  (dbx) assign db_enable = 0

This fixes the working copy in memory.  If this stops the crash from
occurring you can then patch the /vmunix file:

  # dbx /vmunix                     /* do NOT use -k, we want the disk
copy
  (dbx) patch db_enable = 0
  (dbx) q

I don't know if an "official" patch exists for this.  I took a quick
look
on the Digitual Unix home page (http://www.unix.digital.com), but did
not
see it.


Pat

