peter@asimov.oit.umass.edu (Peter deFriesse)
Peter deFriesse from 'The Gang of "Peters"' wrote:

> Forget the commercial crap:  Do a search for psf (the PostScript
> Filter).  It is free and very powerful.  I'm using psf-3.6 with
> no problems.

Great! But not as a runtime filter. Look at the documentation of psf.

"   psflpd       is used by the lpr/lpd spooling system. It
                accepts input only on stdin and cannot accept any
                run-time arguments, not even those passed by lpd."

So, either you have to put up seperate queues for different 
functionalities (worse than 2 queues for raw/text), or every time 
you have to run it from command line for each function you need.

Also, psf cannot be used to print directly to network printer over
TCP/IP. 

nwprint accepts the arguments passwd by lpd and allows printing
to network printers (or serial printers). The single binary can
handle both network printers and serial printers. How about
downloading fonts to printer at power up from a UNIX host? 
nwprint is the only filter I have found that can do that. 



