Adriano Nagelschmidt Rodrigues <anr@ime.usp.br> writes:

>Hello,

>I have a server running OSF1 V3.2 and am having problems with mountd. When
>some client programs test to see if nfs is available, ie they do

>% rpcinfo -u my.osf.server mountd >/dev/null

>They get the reply

>rpcinfo: RPC: Program/version mismatch; low version = 1, high version = 1

>since it's a non-null string, they stop and say nfs service is
>unavailble. Otherwise, mountd works ok (I have already applied the latest
>security patches, but it didn't solve this particular problem).

The problem is that we don't support MOUNT V2 (I forget what, if anything,
that had of interest).

A tcpdump trace of "rpcinfo -u server mountd" follows.

client.f732830f > server.pmap-v2: 56 call getport prog "mount"  V0  prot UDP  port 0
server.f732830f > client.pmap-v2: 28 reply getport 1032
client.f832830f > server.mount-v0: 40 call null
server.f832830f > client.mount-v0: 32 reply RPC_PROGVERSMISMATCH error, low 1 high 3
client.f932830f > server.pmap-v2: 56 call getport prog "mount"  V1  prot UDP  port 0
server.f932830f > client.pmap-v2: 28 reply getport 1032
client.fa32830f > server.mount-v1: 40 call null
server.fa32830f > client.mount-v1: 24 reply null
client.fb32830f > server.pmap-v2: 56 call getport prog "mount"  V2  prot UDP  port 0
server.fb32830f > client.pmap-v2: 28 reply getport 1032
client.fc32830f > server.mount-v2: 40 call null
server.fc32830f > client.mount-v2: 32 reply RPC_PROGVERSMISMATCH error, low 1 high 1
client.fd32830f > server.pmap-v2: 56 call getport prog "mount"  V3  prot UDP  port 0
server.fd32830f > client.pmap-v2: 28 reply getport 1032
client.fe32830f > server.mount-v3: 40 call null
server.fe32830f > client.mount-v3: 24 reply null

You might try "rpcinfo -u server mountd 3", which is the MOUNT version used
in NFS V3 or "rpcinfo -u server mountd 1" which tests for NFS V2.  All servers
I know that support NFS V3 will support NFS V2 for the indefinite future too.

It's also a shorter exchange:

client.3647890f > server.pmap-v2: 56 call getport prog "mount"  V3  prot UDP  port 0
server.3647890f > client.pmap-v2: 28 reply getport 1032
client.3747890f > server.mount-v3: 40 call null
server.3747890f > client.mount-v3: 24 reply null

>Is there a new mountd that corrects the mountd portmapper registration?

It's not a mountd problem.  It may be a portmap problem.  On the other hand,
portmap may be trying to tell the client what it can fall back to.
