Eric Werme <werme@zk3.dec.com> wrote:
>acc@beavis.dei.isep.ipp.pt (Antonio Costa) writes:
>>We have an DECalpha 4/100 266 running DUNIX 3.2C (with some patches from
>>DIGITAL).
>>Some time ago we put a P5/166 UW-SCSI2 Linux machine as our main NIS/NFS
>>server (NFS V2).
>
>While I am fond of Linux, I tried talking to a Linux NFS server at the
>recent Connectathon and was not impressed.  Only when I killed nfsiod
>could I a coax reasonable performance out of it (I think we were swamping
>its input socket).  

We run Linux 2.0 on the main University web server (100000 hits/day
at the moment), a Pentium 133MHz with 64Mb RAM and it NFS exports its
filestore to 20000 users on two Alphaserver 2100s. We get 500-700K/s
for NFS writes to the web server and 700K/s for NFS reads. This is
with the standard user-mode Linux NFS server not the new in-kernel
one which is currently in development. That performance isn't
incredibly fast but it's acceptable in our environment.

>		     Someone from Sun noticed the Linux server also
>reused a file handle, something very, very evil in a NFS server, and
>something that has caused panics in DEC clients in the past (except I
>thought we had fixed that by V3.2C).

Remember the mantra: you shouldn't be able to bring down an NFS
client just because an NFS server sends it dodgy data, *especially*
if, like Ultrix and Digital UNIX, you allow ordinary users to NFS
mount filesystems from remote servers. Digital UNIX 3.2C does not
fix all NFS problems. We ran into one some months ago where a user
mounted a filesystem onto one of our Alphaservers (Digital UNIX 4.0)
from another Linux NFS server somewhere in the University. The
Alphaserver panicked and crashed. DEC sent us a kernel patch last
week (at last) that's supposed to fix it. Notice that since the
Linux NFS server is user-mode, it can't panic the Linux kernel in
that way so you could say we're trading a slight performance drop
(1Mb/s -> 600K/s) for the added reliability of using Linux as an
NFS server instead of Digital UNIX :-)

>I suggest you talk to your customer support people about the Alpha
>crashes, but I would strongly discourage using Linux (whatever
>version I was running against) as a NFS server for files you actually
>expect to reuse.  

As someone who uses and likes both Linux and Digital UNIX a lot,
I'd say to consider Linux for a given solution just as you would
consider any other Unix. If you want maximum NFS performance over
100Mbs or memory channel then Linux can't (yet) compete. On the
other hand if you need the capabilities of Linux where Digital UNIX
(currently) falls short (price/performance, easy built-in
firewalling, faster security fixes, uniformity over multiple
architectures) then that may weigh things against Digital UNIX.
Obviously, the *BSD crowd should be considered equally as should
anything else that will do the job: this isn't Linux advocacy for
the sake of it.

--Malcolm

