I passed on your post to someone who passed it on to someone who can
rattle cages.  No promises, though.

>  After some dinking around, I found that setting the client
>systems to use nfsv2 resolved the problem.  Response was fast
>on the client systems and the load average on the server went
>below zero (like 0.14 or so).

The two major differences between V2 and V3 that tend to affect performance
are "safe asynchronous writes" and READDIRPLUS.  The former should speed up
AdvFS, but will use more VM.  The latter has been an issue for sites with
very large directories that ar commonly used.  (University user directories
are the the classic problem).  What happens is that READDIRPLUS is much
like "ls -l" and returns both file names and attributes.  NFS V2 uses
READDIR, which is like "ls" and only returns names.

One recent performance issue involved Solaris and AdvFS, and what happened
to Solaris's sequential access pattern as it tried to do 32 KB I/O 8 KB
at a time.  Shouldn't be a problem here, as Ultrix does 8KB I/O.

If you have any nfsstat or tcpdump data from then, I'd like to look at
it.  Can't give you too much time, though.  vmstat too!

I'd lean toward the big directories, though even there, I'd expect things
like logging over a V2 mount to issue READDIRs and many lookups as shell
startup does the equivalent of "pwd".  Generally the best thing that comes
from disabling READDIRPLUS is that filename completion is faster.
