In article <332ECF1F.12E0@staffs.ac.uk>, Richard Rogers <R.M.Rogers@staffs.ac.uk> writes:
|> I would be grateful to anyone who can enlighten me about the output
|> from netstat -H. In particular, what are the meanings of the flags
|> in the Flags column, and what exactly is the Timer column telling
|> me?
|> 
|> Before you tell me to RTFM, I have. It says that netstat -H behaves like
|> arp -a; the output is quite different though.

"Behaves like" is perhaps misleading.  It would be more accurate to
say something like "provides information about the same set of
ARP table entries as does arp -a".  However, it provides the
information in a different format, and adds one or two additional
fields not printed by arp -a.

E.g.,

% arp -a
XXXX.pa.dec.com (16.1.99.1) at aa-00-04-00-55-2b
YYYY.pa.dec.com (16.1.99.209) at 08-00-2b-38-10-cc
% netstat -H
Address Resolution Mapping Table: 37 Buckets 16 Deep 48 Bytes
Cnt  Flags    Timer Host                     Phys Addr
  4: UC          3  XXXX                     aa-00-04-00-55-2b
  5: UC          0  YYYY                     08-00-2b-38-10-cc
%

If you want the IP addresses, not host names, use "netstat -Hn".
Sorry, I don't know how to get "netstat -H" to print fully
qualified domain names.

If you don't know what "Cnt" and "Timer" mean, then you probably
don't need to know.  Otherwise, the description of the ARP
table in TCP/IP Illustrated Volume 2 should suffice.

-Jeff




