X-Deja-AN: 226776258
References: <5gn091$he9$1@lhc.nlm.nih.gov> <332F39B6.41C6@lsil.com> <5gove4$fj5$1@lhc.nlm.nih.gov>
To: Jonathan Epstein <epstein@gold.nlm.nih.gov>
Organization: Digital UNIX Development Environment Group
Newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1


Jonathan Epstein wrote:
> 
> Many thanks, I got a clean link, and have asked the 3.2 user to try it ...
> hopefully this will work.
> 
> - Jonathan
> 
> Bill Deegan (deegan@lsil.com) wrote:
> > ......
> > > dnet_conn
> > >
> > > I have confirmed that these symbols are not mine, but I have thus far
> > > been unable to locate them in the system libraries.
> > >
> > > In principle, is it possible to run applications compiled under version
> > > 4.0 when running on a 3.2 system?  If so, how?

You may get it to work, but it isn't supported.  Going the other way
(i.e. link on V3.2 and run on V4.0 or greater) _IS_ supported.

I know of at least one case where linking something -non_shared on V4.0
will 
NOT run on V3.2, no matter what you do.    In V4.0 the pathconf()
function
was changed from a libc routine to a system call.   If your application 
(or any library you link against) uses pathconf() then you'll get an 
'illegal system call' error when you try to run that application on
V3.2.


