I suggest that you try the unlimit command.  The system comes with some
process limits defaulted to allow 128 MB of memory to be allocated and a
larger amount as a hard limit.  The unlimit command will allow your
process(es) to use the hard limit of the system instead of the soft
limit.  This is a built-in csh function.  If you use another shell, I'm
not sure how to do this.

  A quick test:

1)  type in limit
2)  look for the line which starts with       memoryuse
3)  type in umlimit
4)  memoryuse should show an increase

A couple of caveats:
  I believe it isn't a generally good idea to always do this.  It
allocates kernel space when you do.  So, I'd only do this when what you
are attempting to do requires more memory allocated than the system
default.  
  Changing the default requires a kernel rebuild and a reboot.

Rich O'Keeffe
okeeffe@eng.pko.dec.com


