>DIGITAL).
>Some time ago we put a P5/166 UW-SCSI2 Linux machine as our main NIS/NFS
>server (NFS V2).

While I am fond of Linux, I tried talking to a Linux NFS server at the
recent Connectathon and was not impressed.  Only when I killed nfsiod
could I a coax reasonable performance out of it (I think we were swamping
its input socket).  Someone from Sun noticed the Linux server also
reused a file handle, something very, very evil in a NFS server, and
something that has caused panics in DEC clients in the past (except I
thought we had fixed that by V3.2C).

I suggest you talk to your customer support people about the Alpha
crashes, but I would strongly discourage using Linux (whatever
version I was running against) as a NFS server for files you actually
expect to reuse.  Working on the Linux NFS server could be a fun
pasttime, unfortunately I don't have spare time for it.

You might do better with the Alpha box as the server - that way you'd
also get a fast V3 server.
