From: Craig Neth USG <neth@zk3.dec.com>
Subject: Re: Wrappers for shared library functions?
Date: 1997/03/11
Message-ID: <3325B36A.41C6@zk3.dec.com>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 224738897
References: <331D5BCE.5329@netcomuk.co.uk>
To: john hall <jhall@netcomuk.co.uk>
Organization: Digital UNIX Development Environment Group
Newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1


john hall wrote:
> 
> I would be very grateful for any advice on how to create "wrappers" for
> one or two functions in large shared libraries, such that a calling
> program will invoke the wrapper function which in turn calls the original
> function to do the majority of the "work".

One way to do this would be to write the wrappers to use
dlopen()/dlsym()
to open the library that contains the symbol in question and then have
the
wrapper call the function.  

For example, suppose I wanted to provide a 'sqrt()' wrapper that printed
a message to stdout every time sqrt() was called:


hook.c:
-----------------------------------------------
#include <stdio.h>
#include <dlfcn.h>

double sqrt(double x)
{
  void *handle;
  double (*sqrt_sym)();
  double result;

  handle = dlopen("/usr/shlib/libm.so", RTLD_LAZY);
  sqrt_sym = dlsym(handle, "sqrt");
  printf ("calling sqrt\n");
  result = (*sqrt_sym) (x);
  dlclose(handle);
  return (result);
}

Build this function in a .so file, say hook.so:

 cc -shared -o hook.so hook.o  /* this works on V4.0 or greater. 
Pre-V4.0, use 'ld' */

Now link your program against hook.so:

 cc -o sqrt sqrt.c hook.so -lm

Now, at this point, you haven't hooked anything yet.   You need one
more thing:

 setenv _RLD_LIST  hook.so:DEFAULT

This tells the loader to always load your library before any others.
(If hook.so is not in your LD_LIBRARY_PATH you'll need to add that
before running
this example).

What happens is that because the hook library is loaded first, the calls
in sqrt.c to sqrt() will get resolved to the one in the hook library.    
The hooked sqrt() implementation will find the 'real' sqrt() function in
libm and
call it directly.

You need to be careful doing things like this.    Some functions in the
standard
libraries are implemented as a 'family' of routines that share static
data.   If
you preempt one of the routines and change it's implementation, but
don't also
preempt the other symbols in the family, then you could have all sorts
of nasty
problems. (malloc() is one example of such a 'family' of routines)  
Also, 
there are no guarantees that you will catch *all* calls to a given entry
point 
this way - for example, internal calls in a library may or may not call
the
function in such a way that you can preempt it.   

Craig

