In article <33257FBB.41C6@osf.org>, Aamir Rashid <aamir@osf.org> wrote:

>I have successfully installed 'xmcd' (a cdplayer for digital unix) 
>from http://sunsite.unc.edu/~cddb/xmcd/ on my system. When I try to
>start the application, I get the following error:
>
>8919:/usr/bin/X11/xmcd: /sbin/loader: Fatal Error: cannot map libSM.so
>
>I cannot find 'libSM.so' anywhere. Should'nt the install program
>install this library somewhere ? Does anyone know how to fix this
>problems ?
>
>Thanks.
>-aamir

libSM.so is one of the X shared libraries supplied with Digital UNIX 4.0. 
If the xmcd executable is looking for it, that means whoever built this
xmcd built it on a 4.0 system.  You're getting this error almost certainly
because you have  a Digital UNIX 3.2x system, where libSM.so isn't part of
the X distribution.  In general many executables built on 4.0 probably
won't work on 3.2x systems because of problems like these.  3.2x
executables mostly do run on 4.0 systems however.  So there is backward,
but not forward compatibility.  The only simple solutions are:

1.  Find an xmcd built on a 3.2x system.
2.  Get the source of xmcd from the same site and build it yourself on
your 3.2x system.  As I recall it is fairly straightforward for anyone who
has done any UNIX programming before.

--Tony
tquan@ibm.net



