john hall wrote:
>
> One point on which I am uncertain is what happens to bad block
> tables and the like. Are they invisible and transparent to users of
> the raw SCSI device file or will the target disk acquire the same
> bad block data as the source, which will likely be completely
> inappropriate.

I received a private e-mail response on this which I reproduce below
for the information of the newsgroup. Thanks PR!

-BEGIN QUOTE----------
On SCSI devices, the bad block areas are reserved and managed by the
firmware on the device, transparently to the system.  On Digital UNIX, the
scu command gives you access to the bad block remapping info if you so
desire to look at it.

A dd of the device will address the blocks on the device using logical
block numbering, and the device automagically performs any bad block
remapping necessary.

So, go ahead and dd.
-END QUOTE ----------

regards
john hall
jhall@netcomuk.co.uk




