: Slavisa Lukic wrote:
: > 
: > I have installed a deskjet 1600CM printer as text. I made the entries in
: > the printcap according to the suggestion made in some previous messages
: > of this news group. I have defined two printers (raw+text).
: > 
: > It seems that everything functions well, except that some options of the
: > lpr command are not accepted. I have tried the -Olandscape by the text
: > come still portrait.
: 
: The -O option (alongs with some others) is not necessarily supported on
: other vendor's lpd systems - it's a Digital thing, so if the remote
: printer is not on a Digital system it's unlikely to know what to do.

: I'm currently working on a distributed printing system and have
: encountered these problems, although in my case I'm using a Digital
: Alpha to do the printing and adding code to the standard BSD lpd source
: to give it the extra functionality when passing jobs to the Alpha's lpd
: (i.e. the reverse of what you want and much easier!). I don't know how
: the Digital lpd actually implements things like orientation (-O) and
: paging (-K)...

The lack of filtering by printing remote is usual in most unix (lpd) types.
This can't be elegant overided by a old but not well known trick:

make a printqueue which print raw to the remote printer

make multiple printqueues for the features you want have 
eg one called landscape 

this should have this entries in the printcap:
	:if=/usr/local/daemon/filter/input_filter_landscape:\
        :lp=/dev/null:\

make the filter input_filter_landscape

(echo "here the esc-sequences for initialising the printer & landscape mode"
 cat - ) | lpr -Praw

thats all
you can make this queues if you want on only one machine and print remote
to this machine.

No fiddeling with lpd source code.

Juergen
  



