Sorry but it is not enough to delete the esl0 device, because it is recreated after rebooting
the system ;-(. So you must completly remove the O3D kit and reinstall  the necessary
stuff. If dxsysinfo crashes after deleting the esl0 device, check your scsi bus for termination
and remove all external read-only devices.

It is possible to use special parameters for dxsysinfo to jump over the automatic testing
procedure and to check which part of your configuration get wrong. ;-)

The esl0 driver is for Ether & Sutherland graphic cards which communicates via a special bus
interface ($100.000 per card) It is only possible to detect the card by writing init stuff to the
created device.

Sorry it is not a poor software design because the hardware dependant drivers must be
configured by the system administrator e.g. you must know what you do.......
(to get NT ready you need no special skills so it is choosen from all the well known "experts".......
but try to develop programs or use multiuser applications and you'll really happy with unix.)






