Peter Chapin wrote:
> 
> I have found that the program /usr/bin/X11/dxsysinfo crashes my system
> with total reliability. It causes an "Illegal memory reference in kernel
> mode." The crash occurs as the program is initializing. I get no change to
> interact with it.
> 
> This occured the first time a few hours after I first installed my system.
> I was just trying things out. It has occured on several other occasions
> when students similary just tried it out. I have removed execute
> permission on that program for now, but I'm curious if there is some easy
> explaination for this problem and/or patch available. Has anyone else seen
> this?
> 
> I'm running Digital Unix v4.0A on an Alphaserver 400. I'm running CDE.
> 
> *****************************************************************************
> Peter                                    http://twilight.vtc.vsc.edu/~pchapin
> pchapin@twilight.vtc.vsc.edu      Paganism: Ancient beliefs in a modern world


The problem you are experiencing is not actually caused by the 
dxsysinfo application. The problem is that there is a bad device
driver installed that is really causing the Kernel panic. 

I'm not sure of the actual driver, but I think this driver is part
of the OpenGL system. Do you have the Open3D system on your machine?

To prove that the dxsysinfo app is not the culprit, the following 
command will also cause your system to kernal panic:

	file /dev/*

If your system kernel paniced by using sysinfo, then it should also
crash with this command. You may need to execute the command as root,
but I'm not 100% sure, try it both as root and non-root.

The sysinfo app in trying to determine the devices, goes out and
does an open() and ioctl() system call on each device in /dev.
When it hits the bad device driver, boom, kernel panic. 

Now, since the driver is not on every machine, not every machine
experiences this problem. So, the root of this problem is a bad
device driver not dxsysinfo, but by running "Update Devices" in
dxsysinfo, the problem becomes apparent.

I think Digital has a patch for the OpenGL driver. You should 
contact them about this.

Good Luck.

- Phil



