Richard Powell wrote:
> 
> Peter Chapin wrote:
> >
> > I have found that the program /usr/bin/X11/dxsysinfo crashes my system
> > with total reliability. It causes an "Illegal memory reference in kernel
> > mode." The crash occurs as the program is initializing. I get no change to
> > interact with it.
> 
> This is a known bug with Digital 4.0A.
> To get around that is to create a /.sysman directory with the dxsysinfo
> file.   I can't remember the syntax.   Try to run dxsysinfo as a regular
> user,
> and it will create the directory in that user's home dir.   Copy that to
> the
> root dir, and then it won't crash anymore.
> 
> I believe it's fixed in 4.0C, possibly 4.0B as well.
> 
> Good luck,
> angela


The temporary workaround your describing is to create a ~/.sysman/Dxsysinfo 
file that contains the device info. With this file present, dxsysinfo does not 
go out and "hit" (open() and ioctl()) the /dev directory, therefore the problem
does not occur. BUT, if you select "Update Devices", you force the app to obtain 
device info rather than reading it from the ~/.sysman/Dxsysinfo file, and thus
the kernel panic occurs. The underlying problem is not dxsysinfo but a bad
device driver, I think its an Open3D driver.

I think it is either a faulty driver, or that the driver is installed when
it shouldn't be. I suggest you contact Digital about this. Actually, a posting
from - Ralph - seems to indicate that the problem is that the O3D Kit drivers are
installed when they shouldn't be.


- Phil



