: libRenamedReal.so) is found by ld when creating the "dummy" libReal.so,
: the dependencies of the dummy library (revealed through odump -Dl)
: include the name "libReal.so" instead of "libRenamedReal.so". 

Sure enough... I just did:

cp /usr/shlib/libm.so /tmp
vi /tmp/libm.so

And there's libc.so in a list of other (guess) dependancies.  Given that
the start point of the "l" in libc.so must be left in place, and there's
strings immediately following, your ownly choice of hacking libm.so or
libxxx.so for that mater is to make the dependancy on libc.so to be 
libC.so.  Where you just change the characters of the dependant library.

Therefore do:

- Make your wrapper library named:	libm.so  (or what ever you're wrapping)
- Make the real library named:		libM.so
- Edit libM.so to make the dependancies
  on libm.so to be libM.so.

Good luck.


