In article <331CEAED.519A@soback.kornet.nm.kr>, Younghan Kim
<brbin03@soback.kornet.nm.kr> wrote:

>   .. domain1
>        # showfile -x M-6       .... Error messaged displayed
>
>       showfile : lstat failed for file M-6 : No suc file or directory
>
>
>*** Questions
>   1. man page shows that we can adjust extents count by addvol command
>      but after adjusting we met error described already.
>      Why?

/domain_mount_point/.tags/M-6 is valid for Vol 1,  If you did an rmvol for
vol 1, then it doesn't exist any longer, and you have some other volume
numbers assigned.

First you should look at the list of volumes and the vol # assigned before
you attempt to use the M-6 notation.

    showfdmn domain
                   Id              Date Created  LogPgs  Domain Name
    305f197f.000730b1  Tue Sep 19 15:26:55 1995     512  advfs

      Vol   512-Blks        Free  % Used  Cmode  Rblks  Wblks  Vol Name
       1L     715600      203680     72%     on    128    128  /dev/rz2d
       3     1191936      337632     72%     on    128    128  /dev/rz5d
       4      715600      202912     72%     on    128    128  /dev/rz2e
       5      715600      200944     72%     on    128    128  /dev/rz2f
       6      715600      201312     72%     on    128    128  /dev/rz2g
       7     1191936      341072     71%     on    128    128  /dev/rz5e
       8     1194128      337152     72%     on    128    128  /dev/rz5f
      11      715600      199968     72%     on    128    128  /dev/rz2h
          ----------  ----------  ------
             7156000     2024672     72%

You will notice that for this domain, I have no vol 2, vol 9 or vol 10.  So
if I wanted to look at the BMTs for these volumes I would use

    cd /domain_mount_point/.tags
    showfile -x M-6     # vol 1
    showfile -x M-18    # vol 3
    showfile -x M-24    # vol 4
    showfile -x M-30    # vol 5
    showfile -x M-36    # vol 6
    showfile -x M-42    # vol 7
    showfile -x M-48    # vol 8
    showfile -x M-66    # vol 11

You talk the volume number and multiply by 6 to get the BMT file name for
each volume.  

You got an error, so you must not have volume 1.  Look at the showfdmn
output to see what volume numbers are currently being used. 

And your volume numbers you have will depend on your usage of addvol and
rmvol over the life of the domain.

-- 
