November 1996 V1.0

Digital UNIX Software Kits

Releases of Digital UNIX are structured and distributed as full or
sparse
inventory kits.

    Full Inventory Digital UNIX Kit
    -------------------------------
    This type of kit contains a full inventory of operating system
objects
    (headers, libraries, kernel modules, etc.). It can be used to
perform
    two types of installations - Full (also called new) Installations
and
    Update Installations.

        - A full (or new) installation creates new file systems and
loads a
          full copy of Digital UNIX from the kit onto a system. Any
other
          version of Digital UNIX, any layered products, and any patches
which
          may previously have existed on the system are overwritten. A
full
          installation does not preserve system customizations (that is,
user
          or data files) because the root, usr, and var file systems are
          recreated during the process.

        - An update installation from a full inventory kit loads a full
copy
          of Digital UNIX from the kit, replacing every operating system
          object that might have existed on the system prior to the
          installation.  An update installation does not update layered
          products. This may cause a regression in operation of a
layered
          product if a layered product version of a Digital UNIX object
is
          replaced with a new version of that object.

    The end result of either a full or an update installation is an
operating
    system consisting of a known set of operating system objects that
provides
    predictable system behavior.

    Following an update installation it is necessary to install all
layered
    products and all Digital UNIX patches (official as well as test)
that were
    built for the new release.


    Sparse Inventory Digital UNIX Installation
    ------------------------------------------
    The Digital UNIX Version 3.2C family sparse inventory operating
system
    kits do not contain a full inventory of operating system objects.
Also,
    it does not use either the full or the update installation processes
    described above; it uses setld directly.

    Since a sparse inventory kit contains only a partial inventory of
    Digital UNIX objects, installing from this type of kit does not load
an
    entire copy of Digital UNIX onto a system. Existing objects are
    overwritten only if replacement objects exist on the software kit.

    Sparse inventory kits are produced assuming that any system to be
    upgraded is running the baseline Digital UNIX operating system
objects
    from a previous release. The presence of patches, a layered product
that
    modifies base operating system files, etc. cause the system to
deviate
    from one of the supported baselines and has the potential to cause
    object inconsistency following an installation from a sparse
inventory
    kit. Therefore, special care must be exercised when upgrading
Digital
    UNIX via a sparse inventory kit. The following tables provide
upgrade
    information for the V3.2, V3.2C, and V4.0 families of releases.

    Following an sparse inventory installation, all appropriate versions
of
    layered products must be installed and all Digital UNIX patches
    (official as well as test) that were built for the new release.
Failure
    to do so will probably cause a regression in the behavior of layered
    products, Digital UNIX, or both.


